// src/std/core/range.zen
// what every loop word walks.
//
// DESIGN.md types the loop family's first parameter `range: Range`,
// passing a fixed array, a Vec, a Map — without ever declaring Range.
// One name carries both readings:
//
//   CONCRETE: `Range(0, 5)` constructs one — half-open storage, so
//   five passes (the off-by-one that makes it six has a corpus test).
//
//   A BOUND: an ordinary struct used as a bound (DESIGN.md: "a bound
//   is just which struct's shape do I need"). A Vec satisfies it as
//   Circle satisfies Rect — supplying values for fields it doesn't store.
//
// So the family is `<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, ..)`, not
// `(range: Range<T>, ..)`: an interface-TYPED param is a fat value
// carrying function pointers, not a plain C for-loop. A bound
// monomorphises instead — `at` is a direct call, the body inlines,
// and tests/bench/bench_loop.zen gates a fold at allocs_op: 0. Nothing
// is impl'd here: a trait sits BELOW the types that satisfy it — the
// two impls this file needs live with their types, named at the bottom.

Res = std.core.result

// a half-open index interval [start, end), plus what lives at an
// index. `at` is the ONE thing an impl supplies; a None ends the walk
// early, so a container whose length moved under the loop stops rather
// than reading past itself. Optional hook (`::=`, no body): supplying
// none walks the index space itself — `Range(0, v.len)` is the
// ordinary way to walk indices (how std.collections uses it).
Range*<T> = {
    start*: usize,
    end*: usize,

    at* ::= (self: @Self, index: usize) Res<T>
}

// THE TWO IMPLS THIS FILE SPECIFIES AND DOES NOT OWN
//
// A VEC is a Range of its elements — one line, since `get` already
// answers what `at` asks, including the None past the end. Lives in
// std.collections.collections_vec, beside the type:
//
//     Vec.impl(Range<T>, {
//         start: 0,
//         end: self.len,
//         at ::= (self: @Self, index: usize) Res<T> { self.get(index) }
//     })
// start/end are supplied, not stored, so growing mid-loop reports the
// new end, not a stale one.
//
// A FIXED ARRAY satisfies Range intrinsically — the one place the
// compiler supplies what no module could write: `[T, N]` is a type
// constructor, not a name, so there's no `[T, N].impl(..)` to write.
// Its start is 0, end is the comptime length, `at` the bounds-checked
// index that already traps.
